---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="How I Work | Calileus" description="How Calileus designs C++ software, tests it, and helps teams work together." path="/architecture">
  <main>
    <section class="page-intro mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24" aria-labelledby="architecture-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">HOW I WORK</p>
      <h1 id="architecture-heading">Simple designs are easier to change.</h1>
      <p>I use clear C++ interfaces, small components, and automatic checks to keep software understandable.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section-band mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6" aria-labelledby="capabilities-heading">
      <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">01 / WHAT I DO</p><h2 id="capabilities-heading">The main parts of the work.</h2><p>Good boundaries help people work on a system without getting in each other's way.</p></div>
      <div class="capability-grid">
        <article class="capability-card"><span class="capability-number">01</span><h3>Components</h3><p>Separate pieces with clear jobs, stable interfaces, and builds that work on Windows and Linux.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>C++20</span><span>Protobuf</span><span>Linux / Windows</span></div></article>
        <article class="capability-card"><span class="capability-number">02</span><h3>Data pipelines</h3><p>Code that turns radar detections into tracked and classified objects for customers.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>Events</span><span>FMI</span><span>Radar</span></div></article>
        <article class="capability-card"><span class="capability-number">03</span><h3>Reliable software</h3><p>Checks, reviews, and tests for automotive code where mistakes are expensive.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>MISRA C/C++</span><span>Static analysis</span><span>ADAS</span></div></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-band mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6" aria-labelledby="philosophy-heading">
      <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">02 / PRINCIPLES</p><h2 id="philosophy-heading">Clarity beats cleverness.</h2><p>Software is easier to maintain when the next person can understand it.</p></div>
      <div class="philosophy-panel"><p>I try to make the right way to change a system easy to see.</p><ul class="philosophy-list"><li><strong>Clear</strong> — Small parts and explicit decisions.</li><li><strong>Testable</strong> — Components that can be checked on their own.</li><li><strong>Shared</strong> — Simple rules that help the whole team contribute.</li></ul><p>That matters even more in automotive software, where dependable behavior is part of the job.</p></div>
    </section>

    <section class="section-band mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6" aria-labelledby="quality-heading">
      <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">03 / CHECKS</p><h2 id="quality-heading">Let the tools catch the easy mistakes.</h2><p>Automatic checks leave more time for the hard decisions.</p></div>
      <div class="capability-grid"><article class="capability-card"><h3>Builds and tests</h3><p>Every change is built and tested automatically.</p></article><article class="capability-card"><h3>Code checks</h3><p>Formatting and C++ rules are checked before review.</p></article><article class="capability-card"><h3>Releases</h3><p>Versions and builds follow a clear, repeatable process.</p></article></div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>